{"text": " It's the fastest growing thing the United States buys, and it buys nothing. Interest on the federal debt already runs more than a trillion dollars a year. That's more than the country spends on defense or veterans benefits. And by the Congressional Budget Office's own numbers, it passes Medicare by 2028. When government debt comes due, the government doesn't pay it off with tax revenue or a surplus. That would be too fiscally responsible. Instead, it just borrows the money again. Same debt, new bond, at today's rate. We pay the old debt by issuing new debt. The average interest rate across everything America owes is sitting around three and a half percent That's low and it's low because most of it was borrowed back when money was cheap But nothing being sold today costs three and a half percent Short-term bills are around four percent while 30-year money runs around five point two and roughly a third of America's tradable debt comes Due over the next 12 months more than ten trillion dollars which means every dollar of that cheap debt gets replaced at the new price investors are charging today. So every time we roll the debt forward the average creeps up with it one maturity at a time and the interest bill grows. It doesn't take an auction to fail or everybody to panic sell. It just takes time. It's inevitable.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.88, "text": " It's the fastest growing thing the United States buys, and it buys nothing.", "tokens": [50363, 632, 338, 262, 14162, 3957, 1517, 262, 1578, 1829, 24779, 11, 290, 340, 24779, 2147, 13, 50567], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1475232294363569, "compression_ratio": 1.5866261398176291, "no_speech_prob": 0.004854394588619471, "words": [{"word": " It's", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.16, "probability": 0.7918130457401276}, {"word": " the", "start": 0.16, "end": 0.3, "probability": 0.9960513710975647}, {"word": " fastest", "start": 0.3, "end": 0.68, "probability": 0.9898794889450073},